cbor — third-party attribution
====================================================================

This file carries a CONDITION, added 2026-09-09. `src/kat_vectors.zig` and
`src/cose_kat_vectors.zig` reproduce test vectors published in RFC 8949
Appendix A and RFC 9052 Appendix C. Redistributing this module — alone, or as
part of zig-libs — must keep this file with it.

WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW. `README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement
said "RFC 8949/9052 are public specifications (merger doctrine); no NOTICE entry
needed" — an answer about the module's CODE, applied to data it never mentioned.
The gate written for exactly this shape could not see the data: `check-catalog`'s
data-file claim looked for a committed file that is neither Zig source nor
documentation, and these vectors live in `.zig` files, so no witness was found
and the check never ran. That hole was closed on 2026-09-09; this file is one of
the four records it forced.

No code changed, and none needs to. The vectors are unaltered.

WHAT WAS TAKEN
--------------

  - `src/kat_vectors.zig` — the encoded-CBOR examples of RFC 8949 Appendix A
    ("Examples of Encoded CBOR Data Items"), as hex plus the expected decoded
    value.
  - `src/cose_kat_vectors.zig` — `COSE_Sign1` examples from RFC 9052
    Appendix C, including the C.2.1 "Single ECDSA Signature" worked example,
    transcribed byte-for-byte from the RFC text.

⭐ A SECOND SOURCE WAS CONSULTED AND NOTHING WAS TAKEN FROM IT. The Appendix A
transcription was cross-checked against the community repository
`cbor/test-vectors`' `appendix_a.json`, itself a transcription of the same RFC
table. That repository publishes NO licence (GitHub reports none, re-verified
2026-09-09), which is exactly why it is not a source here: the authoritative
text is the RFC, the values below were taken from the RFC, and the repository
served only to confirm the transcription agreed with an independent one. Had
any value been taken from it instead, this module would be reproducing
unlicensed material and the answer would have to be to remove it — see root
`NOTICE` §1.

THE GRANT THIS RESTS ON
-----------------------

Not the merger doctrine. RFC test vectors are Code Components in the sense of
the IETF Trust Legal Provisions (BCP 78) — the Trust's list of Code Components
names "tables of values" explicitly — and TLP 5.0 §4.c grants them under the
Revised BSD License. A written grant is available at no cost, so this module
takes it rather than arguing that a table of values is uncopyrightable. That is
the same choice `modules/mls/NOTICE` made on 2026-09-08 and the one
`modules/ctap2pin/NOTICE` already cited.

